Use High's coding tools, and make the pipeline knobs reachable.

Three things, all measured against an LG webOS sink at 1920x1080@60 over MICE.

gnd-encoder-output.patch declares High in the output caps but upstream leaves
vah264enc's "cabac" and "dct8x8" FALSE, so a stream advertised as High still
codes like Baseline -- the ~15-25% its own header warns about. Enable both, but
only when High is what was actually negotiated.

videoconvert defaults to n-threads=1. The capture is BGRA 2560x1600, roughly
983 MB/s at 60fps through one thread.

The rest are env knobs, because the right value is sink-specific and finding it
by rebuilding is painful:

  GND_SCALE_METHOD     videoscale filter (1 bilinear, 3 lanczos)
  GND_GAMMA_MODE=remap convert the sRGB transfer rather than relabelling it
  GND_COLORIMETRY      whole range:matrix:transfer:primaries string
  GND_TARGET_USAGE     vah264enc effort, 1 best .. 7 fastest
  GND_REF_FRAMES       reference frames
  GND_CONVERT_THREADS  0 = auto

Defaults are what measured best, not what looks best on paper. Both lanczos and
gamma remap halve the framerate on this capture -- GStreamer's latency tracer put
wfd-scale at 2.57ms and wfd-videoconvert at 4.51ms mean already -- and the sharper
scaling was not visible on the sink, so both stay opt-in. With these defaults the
pipeline runs 5972 frames in 100.2s (59.6 fps) at 16.84ms mean end-to-end, with 43
frames of 6000 late and one stall.

Not upstreamed.

